0 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102335 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102335 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a76b867-a0f7-5b70-af29-345e081b2cd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105610 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105610 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ in polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb897f41-dd8e-561f-be0e-02d2f9972203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105610 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(0,a)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|2\\right| = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105611 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105611 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the polar equation of the curve shown below?\n\n![](images/0a/0ad44bfa452da482fff5f579f1be9569670f7ad8da347314bcb63f618eaf3d2a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9958848d-a3ec-599e-bbc4-44b3993bc707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105611 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & =\\end{vmatrix}-4.5 &= 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,-4.5)$.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 2a\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-4.5) \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&=-9\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105613 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105613 #edb/ref 1071 "The polar curve $r=k\\cos\\theta$, where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $\\left(-\\dfrac72,\\dfrac72\\right)$. What is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e0646a3-f4d2-5a29-ac05-519494a06177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105613 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A$. The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{7}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(\\frac{7}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan \\mid - ((\\frac{7}{2}))/((\\frac{7}{2})) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= k\\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} &= k\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4}) \\\\\n\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} &= k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\nk &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\nr=-7\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105669 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105669 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ in polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd2bf9b1-9198-5040-9ef4-9924b4181108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105669 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{2}{2} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|1\\right| = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(1,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc5f763522aec68e97bef90126866fe6b2fe7246560d4dc9ddd2c71a5a524d88.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105758 #edb/ref 1039 "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105758 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105758 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105759 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105759 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105759 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105759 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105759 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105761 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1039 "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105836 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1045 "Polar Equations of Circles Centered on the Coordinate Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105952 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ca486896-39b1-5972-b175-42e6a76d9594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106070 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r = 4\\cos θ$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea96cdbb-5bc6-555b-973f-b15da8fc1288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106073 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r =-4\\cos θ$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c594006f-3fa1-5e7d-916f-d46c6f188a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106074 #edb/ref 1071 "The polar curve $r = k\\cos θ$, where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-1, 1)$. What is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86e1c85f-e8f2-5108-a533-e87dddde9479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106659 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106659 #edb/ref 1061 19877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c75aacbb-02ff-51ce-ba99-d3b55a1383cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106660 #edb/ref 1061 19879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a41da03-9799-5f4c-8366-eca36182172d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106661 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106661 #edb/ref 1061 19880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55bc57c5-e879-57a9-8620-d5ced457c8ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106662 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106662 #edb/ref 1061 19881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106662 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39d55595-51da-541e-8bd9-405d1dcce755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106662 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106664 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106664 #edb/ref 1061 19883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29ea04d0-1517-5ed0-ab95-21b1ab2e1718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106792 #edb/ref 1061 28345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08506d5f-4ac6-5cb7-be27-185b7dd2b066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106923 #edb/ref 1061 464110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a681a0f-8eba-55a1-b05e-b4aa4557b465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106924 #edb/ref 1061 464112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e723ee8a-09d9-58ba-ae10-4ce50061926a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107028 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/46/4632ae0baaced6ba3699fddf859b71226b5fcebe4700bbbdf4774d1d701e53ad.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(a, 0)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(a, 0)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 + r^2\\sin^2\\theta = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we can simplify the above as follows:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\cos\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107028 #edb/ref 1065 13319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107028 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0eb1b8d-075c-5b34-a2c1-5cb900398a10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107028 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Polar Equation of a Circle Centered at (a,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107029 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/a2/a2662be5d74155ca29b600cf48982a1ee2b0db62459334af7784394cabea8687.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(0, a)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(0, a)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - 0)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta + r^2\\sin^2\\theta - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we simplify:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\sin\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107029 #edb/ref 1065 13320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8215bce2-13e6-53d9-a691-505556bd572d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107029 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Polar Equation of a Circle Centered at (0,a)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107086 #edb/ref 1064 "When a circle is not centered at the origin, we can still write its equation in polar form. In fact, the polar equation of a circle is particularly straightforward when it passes through the origin and its center lies on the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, let $a$ be some nonzero real number, and consider a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ with center at the point $(a,0)$.\n\n![](images/46/4632ae0baaced6ba3699fddf859b71226b5fcebe4700bbbdf4774d1d701e53ad.png)\n\nWith a little bit of geometry and trigonometry, it turns out that the equation of this circle in polar form is given by\n\n$$\nr=2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, for instance, if $a=2$, then we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr= 4 \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a=-2$, then we have a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\mid {-2} \\mid = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at $(-2,0)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr= -4 \\cos\\theta\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107086 #edb/ref 1065 3509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107086 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0188970c-f17c-55e7-814d-257c054f1f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107086 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107087 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let $a$ be some nonzero real number, and let's consider a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ whose center is at the point $(0,a)$.\n\n![](images/40/40477171cd880e675656ed0093c26a85ebe02b7939ec9421a1bcc52c763a68fe.png)\n\nNotice that this circle passes through the origin, and its center lies on the $y$-axis.\n\nAgain, using some geometry and trigonometry, it's possible to show that the polar equation of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=2a \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, for instance, if $a=2$, then we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr=4 \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a=-3$, then we have a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\mid {-3} \\mid = 3\n$$\n\ncentered at $(0,-3)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr=-6 \\sin \\theta\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107087 #edb/ref 1065 3510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107087 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce8a6f30-10e5-5d3d-8331-f7a9bd767204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107087 #edb/ref 1067 "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
